#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183890 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef9f522-9802-5d91-8610-1341722d7920" :question/math-academy-id "e-1429" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2 +7x -13$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(x^2 -7x +13)$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x- {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x- {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 +\\dfrac{3}4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 7x - 13 &= - ((x - \\frac{7}{2})^{2} + \\frac{3}{4}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{4} - (x - \\frac{7}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183891 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "b38247c0-cb0a-5fa0-afed-45c7e1ba9e3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1430" :question/problem "Rewrite $2x^2 - 6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nlast:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x^{2} - 3x + \\frac{1}{2}) &= 2((x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{7}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\\n&= 2(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184059 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(x^2 - 6x -1)$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 6x + 1 &= - ((x - 3)^{2} - 10) \\\\\n&= - (x - 3)^{2} + 10 \\\\\n&= 10 - (x - 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184060 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(x^{2} - 10x + 20) &= -2((x - 5)^{2} - 5) \\\\\n&=-2(x - 5)^{2} + (-2) \\cdot (-5) \\\\\n&=-2(x - 5)^{2} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The plus sign inside the square produces linear term $-16k$ after subtraction, opposite the required $+16k$. Its constant also does not match $-8$." :answer/id #uuid "eee6e16d-c441-59c2-ada6-182ccb2357e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38 - (k + 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180796} {:answer/feedback "The binomial is correct, but expanding gives constant $46-64=-18$. The original expanded expression has constant $-8$, so the outside constant must be $56$." :answer/id #uuid "1a06d1d8-cedb-5250-af12-c11e07ef5ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180797} {:answer/feedback "Inside the negative factor, $k^2-16k+8=(k-8)^2-56$. Distributing the negative gives $56-(k-8)^2$." :answer/id #uuid "1cfd568b-37ce-5df5-a9fa-8b73150eeec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180798} {:answer/feedback "This expands to $-k^2+16k-10$. The required constant is $-8$, so the outside value is $64-8=56$, not $54$." :answer/id #uuid "41108c78-a181-5696-9455-3e8d8e60d016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180799} {:answer/feedback "Expanding $(k+8)^2$ makes the linear term $-16k$ after subtraction. The original expression expands to $-k^2+16k-8$, so the binomial must use $k-8$." :answer/id #uuid "6c0eb7e4-a69c-56a4-a6f6-2405cb481407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42 - (k + 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Inside the negative factor, $k^2-16k+8=(k-8)^2-56$. Distributing the negative gives $56-(k-8)^2$." :answer/id #uuid "1cfd568b-37ce-5df5-a9fa-8b73150eeec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56 - (k - 8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180798} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d5b430-77fe-5def-a568-ffee537c9d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180795}] :question/id #uuid "7b217693-bc6b-565e-8cf8-4ff0d3fc9863" :question/math-academy-id "q-112300" :question/problem "Rewrite $- (k^{2} - 16k + 8)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Inside the negative factor, $k^2-16k+8=(k-8)^2-56$. Distributing the negative gives $56-(k-8)^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The negative sign inside the square produces linear term $+6z$ after multiplication by $-9$. The original linear term is $-6z$." :answer/id #uuid "d63b121a-2d4c-5ad7-83a9-37337db28abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z - \\frac{1}{3})^{2} - 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181012} {:answer/feedback "The square gives the correct quadratic and linear terms, but its constant contribution is $-1$. Adding $5$ produces constant $4$, not $9$." :answer/id #uuid "da56f077-8cf7-5157-bbf1-c4135577f469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181013} {:answer/feedback "Factoring $-9$ gives $-9(z^2+\\frac23z-1)$. The inner expression is $(z+\\frac13)^2-\\frac{10}{9}$, yielding $-9(z+\\frac13)^2+10$." :answer/id #uuid "19b4e5a2-f198-5dd9-91ee-22690b49f09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181014} {:answer/feedback "Although the outside constant is correct, the binomial sign is not: this expands with linear term $+6z$ instead of $-6z$." :answer/id #uuid "3d23c4e0-9841-595a-881a-a7142756481a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z - \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181015} {:answer/feedback "The binomial is correct, but the square contributes constant $-1$. Adding $8$ leaves constant $7$, whereas the original constant is $9$." :answer/id #uuid "4bff8dd4-6a62-5533-bda1-ce3db8e0709c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Factoring $-9$ gives $-9(z^2+\\frac23z-1)$. The inner expression is $(z+\\frac13)^2-\\frac{10}{9}$, yielding $-9(z+\\frac13)^2+10$." :answer/id #uuid "19b4e5a2-f198-5dd9-91ee-22690b49f09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9(z + \\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181014} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d311a9-62f1-5a3d-8a34-95dcd2c3b053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181011}] :question/id #uuid "2495fc0b-8e76-56e1-bf72-c223c52c4fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140378" :question/problem "Rewrite $-9z^{2} - 6z + 9$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Factoring $-9$ gives $-9(z^2+\\frac23z-1)$. The inner expression is $(z+\\frac13)^2-\\frac{10}{9}$, yielding $-9(z+\\frac13)^2+10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The negative sign produces linear term $-6y$, whereas the original has $+6y$. The binomial must use $y+1$." :answer/id #uuid "829bb4ab-d1cb-5a64-8e93-4d59d2b34760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y - 1)^{2} + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183372} {:answer/feedback "Factoring $3$ gives $3(y^2+2y+5)$. Since $y^2+2y+5=(y+1)^2+4$, the result is $3(y+1)^2+12$." :answer/id #uuid "bb45789d-be04-56ed-8435-f113628d9cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y + 1)^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183373} {:answer/feedback "This has linear term $-6y$ because the binomial uses $y-1$. It also expands with constant $13$, not $15$." :answer/id #uuid "c0fecf75-2928-5a27-a9d6-6cd9f30be441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y - 1)^{2} + 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183374} {:answer/feedback "Squaring $3y+1$ produces leading term $9y^2$, not $3y^2$. The leading coefficient belongs outside the completed square." :answer/id #uuid "3f3ddcd2-4d11-51f1-a06f-72abf451bbfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y + 1)^{2} + 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183375} {:answer/feedback "The square gives the correct quadratic and linear terms, but its constant contribution is $3$. Adding $5$ yields constant $8$, not $15$." :answer/id #uuid "4a02cb19-bab9-5624-8ca4-bda22c0d6925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y + 1)^{2} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Factoring $3$ gives $3(y^2+2y+5)$. Since $y^2+2y+5=(y+1)^2+4$, the result is $3(y+1)^2+12$." :answer/id #uuid "bb45789d-be04-56ed-8435-f113628d9cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y + 1)^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183373} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd36fead-dbde-5c9f-8a2d-0eaa33c2d514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183371}] :question/id #uuid "5b21db9d-e784-5ce2-96a2-6fd7b3008ee3" :question/math-academy-id "q-52542" :question/problem "Rewrite $3y^{2} + 6y + 15$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Factoring $3$ gives $3(y^2+2y+5)$. Since $y^2+2y+5=(y+1)^2+4$, the result is $3(y+1)^2+12$."}]}
